What is Software Testing: 

Imagine you’re building a brand-new coffee machine 

Before selling it, you’d want to check:
• Does it make coffee when I press the button?
• What if I forget to add water?
• Will it still work after 100 uses?
• Can anyone use it easily?

Software testing is just like that — but for apps, websites, and digital products.

When developers build software, testers step in to:

• Check if everything works as expected
• Catch problems before real users see them
• Try different situations to make sure the product is reliable
• Give feedback so it can be improved

At its core, software testing is the process of evaluating a system to verify that it meets the expected requirements and is free of defects.

But it’s more than that.

It’s about asking critical questions like:
• Does this software do what it’s supposed to do?
• What happens when it’s used incorrectly?
• Can the user trust it to behave reliably every time?

Testing helps teams:
• Prevent costly bugs from reaching users
• Improve user experience and trust
• Ship confidently and move faster

Whether it’s manual, automated, functional, or non-functional, every type of testing contributes to one goal:
Delivering high-quality software that works.

In simple words:

Testing is about ensuring that software behaves correctly, under all expected (and unexpected) conditions.

We’re the team behind the scenes making sure things don’t break — even when you least expect it.

